          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 109,
          "character": 132
        }
      },
      "title": "The buyer's phone sends the scan",
      "description": "### The buyer's phone sends the scan\n\n**DSA topic:** 13. Haversine distance + speed check\n\nWhen a buyer opens a QR link, the phone sends the QR text, a **device id** (a random id saved on that phone) and its **GPS position** to the server.\nEvery check below works on these three things."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/lib/device.ts",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 4,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 8,
          "character": 6
        }
      },
      "title": "How we tell two phones apart",
      "description": "### How we tell two phones apart\n\nThe first time a phone scans, it makes a random id and keeps it. A different phone (or a private window) gets a different id.\nThis is how the server knows that *another* phone scanned the same QR."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/app.ts",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 645,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 650,
          "character": 142
        }
      },
      "title": "The verify endpoint",
      "description": "### The verify endpoint\n\nThe server passes the scan to `verifyScan` with `record: true`, so this scan is saved and later scans are compared with it."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/logic.ts",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 298,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 301,
          "character": 6
        }
      },
      "title": "Check 1: the item was already claimed by another phone",
      "description": "### Check 1: the item was already claimed by another phone\n\n**DSA topic:** 1. SHA-256 hashing\n\nInside every pack there is a **hidden code**. The first genuine buyer types it in, and the server stores the time and that buyer's device id (`claimed_ts`, `claimed_device`).\nIf the item is already claimed and **a different phone** scans it, someone has copied the printed QR. `clone = true` and the reason is written down.\n\n**Data structure:** the hidden code is never stored as plain text. Only its **SHA-256 hash** is stored (see the claim step), so even a database leak does not reveal the codes."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/logic.ts",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 302,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 313,
          "character": 6
        }
      },
      "title": "Check 2: impossible travel since the last scan",
      "description": "### Check 2: impossible travel since the last scan\n\n**DSA topic:** 3. Hash map / hash set, 13. Haversine distance + speed check\n\n`ix.lastSeen` is our own **hash map** (unit id → where and when it was last scanned). Looking up the last scan is O(1) on average, so this check stays fast with millions of items.\n\n`haversineKm` gives the distance on the Earth's surface between the last scan and this one. `impliedSpeedKmh` divides it by the time between the scans.\nIf the item would have had to travel faster than **900 km/h** (`MAX_SPEED_KMH`, faster than a plane), one physical pack cannot be in both places: the QR was copied. Under 5 km is treated as GPS noise."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/logic.ts",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 314,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 328,
          "character": 8
        }
      },
      "title": "Check 3: an unsold item scanned far away from its shop",
      "description": "### Check 3: an unsold item scanned far away from its shop\n\n**DSA topic:** 6. Tree + DFS, 13. Haversine distance + speed check\n\nIf records say the item is still on a retailer's shelf (not sold), it should be near that shop. `effectiveCustody` walks **up the packaging tree** to find who holds it.\nIf it is scanned more than **50 km** (`SHELF_RADIUS_KM`) away, the QR is being used somewhere it cannot be."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/logic.ts",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 330,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 337,
          "character": 6
        }
      },
      "title": "Check 4: too many different phones",
      "description": "### Check 4: too many different phones\n\nCounts how many different phones have scanned this item. More than **5** (`MAX_DEVICES`) for one pack means the QR was photocopied and shared."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/logic.ts",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 338,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 338,
          "character": 71
        }
      },
      "title": "Any failed check → Suspicious clone",
      "description": "### Any failed check → Suspicious clone\n\nIf any of the four checks set `clone = true`, the verdict becomes **SUSPICIOUS_CLONE** and the buyer sees the reasons."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/logic.ts",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 341,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 351,
          "character": 4
        }
      },
      "title": "The scan is saved and an alert is raised",
      "description": "### The scan is saved and an alert is raised\n\n**DSA topic:** 3. Hash map / hash set, 9. Binary heap (priority queue)\n\nThe scan is stored (location rounded to about 1 km for the buyer's privacy), and `ix.lastSeen` is **updated in the hash map**, so the *next* scan is compared with this one. That is why the *second* scan is the one that gets caught.\n\n`raiseAlert` adds a **severity-4 'clone' alert**. Alerts are later ordered with a **binary heap** (see the Alerts tour), so this one shows near the top of the regulator's list. The `dedupe` key stops the same phone from raising the same alert twice."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/app.ts",
